@page "/progress-bar"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>Progress Bar</PageTitle>

<h1>Progress Bar</h1>

<p>Trace of the <em>progress-bar</em> example from <code>HtmlFlow-Datastar-Examples</code>.
    In BRHC, the progress bar is an SSE stream of <code>patchElements</code> - the server
    pushes value updates up to 100%. Here the server (circuit) runs an async loop that
    increments the value and forces a re-render with <code>StateHasChanged</code> - the transport is
    SignalR, not a second channel. <strong>Teaching note:</strong> the "server pushes a sequence
    of states" pattern exists in both stacks; the wire changes (patch SSE vs SignalR diffs).</p>

    <div class="demo-box">
        @if (running)
        {
            <div class="d-flex align-items-center gap-3">
                <div class="progress flex-grow-1" style="height: 24px;">
                    <div class="progress-bar progress-bar-striped progress-bar-animated" style="width: @(progress)%">@progress%
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        }
        else
        {
            <button class="btn btn-primary" @onclick="Start" disabled="@running">Start</button>
            @if (progress >= 100)
            {
                <span class="ms-2 ok">✔ Complete</span>
            }
        }
    </div>

@code {
    private int progress;
    private bool running;

    // Equivalent to the /progress-bar/updates SSE stream: each iteration pushes a new value.
    private async Task Start()
    {
        running = true;
        progress = 0;
        while (progress < 100)
        {
            await Task.Delay(100);
            progress = Math.Min(progress + 5, 100);
            StateHasChanged(); // in BRHC this is a patchElements on the SSE wire
        }
        running = false;
    }
}